Environment Management in Devops concepts 
Environment Management in DevOps
In DevOps, you don't just have one place where your code runs. You have multiple environments that your code passes through before reaching real users. 

The Environments
Environment	            Purpose                                                	        Who uses it
Development (dev)      	Where developers build and test their own code	                Developers
Testing / QA	          Automated and manual testing of integrated code	                QA team
Staging (pre-prod)	    A replica of production for final validation	                  QA + Dev + Product
Production (prod)	      Live environment where real users interact	                    End users

The Promotion Flow
This is where your Git knowledge connects:

dev branch  →  merge to staging  →  merge to main  →  deploy to prod
   (build)         (test)              (approve)         (release)

You build once, then promote the same artifact (same Docker image, same JAR file) through each environment. You never rebuild for production. 


Key Principles
Principle                                	What it means
Infrastructure as Code (IaC)	            Environments are defined in code (Terraform, CloudFormation, Ansible) — not set up manually. Same code → same environment every time.
Containerization	                        Docker / Kubernetes ensure your app runs identically in dev, staging, and prod. No "works on my machine" problems.
Environment Parity	                      Staging should look as close to production as possible (same OS, same config structure, same dependencies) — just smaller scale.
Configuration as Code	                    Environment-specific settings (DB URLs, API keys, feature flags) are stored in version-controlled files or secret managers — never hardcoded.
Prevent Drift	                            "Drift" = when someone manually changes a server and now it differs from the code. IaC + monitoring tools detect and fix this.
Automated Promotion	                      CI/CD pipelines (Jenkins, GitHub Actions, GitLab CI) automatically move artifacts between environments with gates (tests pass, human approval for prod).

