In One Sentence
Environment management in DevOps means defining, provisioning, and promoting your code and infrastructure through a series of increasingly production-like environments — all automated, version-controlled, and consistent — so that what you tested is exactly what ships.


Configuration Management (Deep Dive)
The Problem: Hardcoding
# ❌ BAD — hardcoded
DB_URL = "postgresql://admin:password123@prod-db.example.com:5432/app"
API_KEY = "sk-abc123secret"

Why this is dangerous:

Every environment (dev, staging, prod) needs a different DB URL → you'd have to edit the code every time
If the code leaks (public repo, screenshot, log), all your secrets are exposed
You can't rotate a password without redeploying code
The Fix: Separate Config from Code
# ✅ GOOD — read from environment
import os
DB_URL = os.environ["DB_URL"]
API_KEY = os.environ["API_KEY"]

The same code runs everywhere. Only the values change per environment.

How Values Are Stored
Approach	                      What it is	                                                                  Example
.env files (local dev only)	    Plain-text files, never committed to Git	                                    .env → DB_URL=postgresql://...
CI/CD secrets	                  Stored in the pipeline tool, injected at build/deploy time	                  GitHub Actions → Settings → Secrets
Secret manager (production)	    Dedicated encrypted vault with access control, audit logs, rotation	          HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager
Config files in Git 	          Version-controlled YAML/JSON for non-secret settings	                        config/app.yaml with feature flags
(non-sensitive only)

The Golden Rules
Rule	                                                    Why
Never commit secrets	                                    Use .gitignore for .env; add pre-commit scanners (e.g., gitleaks, trufflehog) to catch accidental leaks
Separate by environment	                                  Prod credentials ≠ dev credentials.  Never share.
Use short-lived / rotating credentials	                  Reduces blast radius if a secret is compromised
Log access, not values	                                  Audit trail of who accessed a secret, without exposing it
Treat config changes like code changes	                  Peer review, version control, rollback capability

Typical Setup in Practice

